the forest is slowly growing back even  starting to outgrow the huts it now  shares these hills with this tree used  to be the only reminder of the thick  canopy cut down to make way for the  hundreds of thousands of reinga who fled  here but what you see here was a forest  reserve three years ago it's taken a  multinational effort by different aid  agencies together with the Bangladeshi  government to host almost 900 thousand  people on this challenging terrain but  what happens now this is the third year  of the Rohingya crisis the first year  was all about saving lives and the  second year was about stabilizing the  situation in the camp that you see and I  would say that the third year really has  to be about preserving people's hope and  their dignity for the year for the  future and when we look at the hills of  Myanmar just over there that's where  people want to go that's what they tell  us they want to repatriate but multiple  attempts to start a voluntary  repatriation program have failed no one  here trusts the Myanmar government will  ensure their safety it hasn't allowed  any observers to visit the rahega areas  and Bangladesh has made it clear it  can't host them forever for the refugees  there is a lot of time to think syed  ahmed is the father of three young  children living here now for almost two  and a half years somehow we are managing  and for that  we are very grateful to all those who  are helping us but we are deeply  concerned about our children's future  and their education although we are  getting by but if we end up staying here  longer then it is very likely our  freedom and movement will be more  restricted there are already  restrictions here the rahega can't leave  the camps nor access the main roads  after dark and recently the military has  started to build a fence around the  camp's perimeter the local Bangladeshi  population is also becoming frustrated  with the refugees this situation is not  sustainable in the long term but there  seems to be little international  pressure on Myanmar to change it it's  almost two and a half years on and still  no one has been held to account but what  happened to the rahaga  these camps are the size of some of the  world's capital cities but where  everyone has a story of tragedy  and trauma it seems the world is simply  accepted that this is just the way it is  this vast landscape refugees hidden away  in far southeastern Bangladesh is simply  a new reality it's a reality no one here  wants to accept with their lives on hold  everyone wants to go home me and Mara's  mountains can be seen from all across  these camps but as things stand for now  it may as well be a world away Stephanie  Decker al-jazeera in the Ranga refugee  camps of southeastern Bangladesh
 

